      "text": "We start with the equation given in the problem: exchanging the larger digit and adding 3 yields $2n$. We then express this algebraically:\n\n`((10a + b- 3)+b)*2 = 2n, b being swapped with restant greater digit: n=(10b+a)/2`  (*)\n\nFor this to be an integer, $10b+a$ must be even, meaning $a$ and $b$ must have the same parity (+ or -). In considering the two-digit numbers from 10 to 99, it's clear that:\n\n- $10-29$ contain an odd two-digit number (e.g., 11, 15, `...`, 29), CAN be a solution but aren't based on parity\n    - Notice that 11 and 29 do have different parity. Another odd two-digit number must satisfy the problem's requirments AND have parity, 12 or `...`, 99 in order.\n- 30-39, uniformly, are even, thus can fulfill the problem's conditions\n    - These numbers have common parity throughout the range of even integers, making 10 to 29 not applicable. Now we can safely consider all numbers up to <number of solutions>\n\nAs shown, we determined all possibilities of $a$ and $b$, which directly reveals that $b$ and $a$ share a common parity and ranges from 5 to 9. We also had a single mathematical requirement: $(10b+a)/2$ must be an integer.     \n\nThus, we convert $b$ and $a$ into integer expressions and apply the condition.\n    \nOnce we apply the common peorthond portable party polynomial equation identity, we conclude that our answer is $n=90$. \nThe final one-liner verifying the solution goes down :\n\nN = 90",
